   SOUTHWESTERN PUBLIC SERVICE COMPANY’S EXPEDITED MOTION FOR
      LEAVE TO FILE LIMITED RESPONSE TO STAFF’S EXCEPTIONS TO
                   CERTIFICATION OF STIPULATION

       Southwestern Public Service Company (“SPS”) requests leave to file, by November 12,

2026, a limited response to Commission Utility Division Staff’s (“Staff”) November 2, 2026,

Exceptions to the Hearing Examiners’ October 23, 2026, Certification of Stipulation

(“Certification”). SPS seeks to only address Staff’s alternative proposal to condition approval of

the Comprehensive Stipulation (“Stipulation”) on additional compliance filings and further

review. Good cause supports this limited request.

       I.      BACKGROUND

       1.      On October 23, 2026, Hearing Examiners Patrick Schaefer and Keven Gedko

issued their Certification, recommending that the Commission approve the Stipulation with

certain modifications.

       2.      SPS and nine intervenors filed the Stipulation on June 22, 2026, to resolve all issues

among the Signatories concerning SPS’s application, including a negotiated $90 million base-rate

increase, effective December 1, 2026. Only Staff opposed the Stipulation.




                                                 1
          3.         On November 2, 2026, SPS and Staff filed exceptions to the Certification. As

 alternative relief, Staff asks the Commission to condition approval of the Stipulation on SPS

 submitting updated financial load forecasts, resource-adequacy information, actual linkage data,

 and related reconciliations before compliance tariffs take effect. Staff further proposes allowing

 Staff and intervenors to comment on these filings, making rates subject to refund, and a delayed

 final Commission decision. 1

         II.        GOOD CAUSE SUPPORTS A LIMITED RESPONSE TO STAFF’S
                    EXCEPTIONS.

         4.         The Commission recently adopted amendments to its procedural rules that

expressly authorize responses to exceptions to certifications of contested stipulations within ten

days, unless otherwise directed. 2 Although these amendments have not yet taken effect because

they have not been published in the New Mexico Register, they reflect the Commission’s policy

favoring an opportunity to respond.

         5.         The Commission’s existing procedural rules also allow responses to exceptions to

recommended decisions within eight days, 3 and no provision prohibits responses to exceptions to

certifications of stipulations.

         6.         Granting leave for SPS to submit a limited response is particularly appropriate here,

where Staff contested the Stipulation through testimony, a four-day evidentiary hearing, and

extensive post-hearing briefing.

         7.         Good cause also exists because Staff’s Exceptions introduce a new proposal that

would impose substantial burdens on SPS. Staff seeks additional filings, further challenges to



         1
             Staff’s Exceptions at 20–21, Conclusion and Requested Relief ¶¶ 2–3
         2
             See 1.2.2.20(C)(6)(b)(iii) NMAC, as adopted in Case No. 23-00119-UT, Final Order, Ex. A (Aug. 27,
2026).
         3
             1.2.2.37(C)(1)(c) NMAC.


                                                         2
 SPS’s revenue requirement and cost allocations, and rates subject to refund after the Commission

approves the Stipulation. Staff identifies no precedent for this procedure or the delays it will cause,

which would effectively turn a tariff filing into another rate case and invite further litigation. SPS

should have an opportunity to address this proposal before the Commission acts.

       8.         Accordingly, SPS requests leave to file a response limited to Staff’s alternative

compliance-filing proposal by November 12, 2026—ten days after Staff filed its Exceptions. This

narrow response would assist the Commission without unnecessarily extending the proceeding.

       9.         SPS requests an expedited ruling on this motion given the impending conclusion of

the suspension period on November 30, 2026.

       10.        SPS has consulted with the other parties regarding this motion, and their positions

are as follows:

             •    New Mexico Department of Justice (NMDOJ): takes no position.

             •    New Mexico Large Customer Group (NMLCG): does not oppose.

             •    Occidental Permian Ltd. (OPL): does not oppose.

             •    Louisiana Energy Services (LES): does not oppose.

             •    Federal Executive Agencies (FEA): does not oppose.

             •    Targa Resources Corp.: does not oppose.

             •    Walmart Inc.: does not oppose.

             •    Coalition for Clean Affordable Energy (CCAE): does not oppose.

             •    Prosperity Works: did not provide position.

             •    New Mexico Energy, Minerals and Natural Resources Department (EMNRD): does

                  not oppose.

             •    Commission Staff: opposes.



                                                   3
        WHEREFORE, SPS respectfully requests that the Commission grant SPS leave to file,

by November 12, 2026, a limited response addressing Staff’s alternative compliance-filing

proposal in its Exceptions to the Certification.



